1
00:00:16,957 --> 00:00:17,897
Good evening.

2
00:00:18,117 --> 00:00:20,120
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:23,083
I do not say happy, though I'm sure it's been happy.

4
00:00:24,004 --> 00:00:40,162
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodassa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodassa

5
00:00:40,884 --> 00:00:48,954
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhassa.

6
00:00:49,515 --> 00:00:58,085
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

7
00:00:58,104 --> 00:01:06,453
So we're still working on this particular discourse, and I just thought it might be useful just to go over these arguments.

8
00:01:06,956 --> 00:01:17,368
Remembering that the Buddha is not being philosophical, he's not being particularly psychological, well, sociological in the sense of morality, I suppose.

9
00:01:18,409 --> 00:01:21,274
His basic angle is always morals.

10
00:01:21,993 --> 00:01:27,260
Now, in an earlier discourse, if you remember, he said he's not good enough just to have purity of morals.

11
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:28,962
Even babies have that.

12
00:01:29,822 --> 00:01:35,530
But what it does is it clears all the turbulence in the mind, the more virtuous we are,

13
00:01:35,915 --> 00:01:38,317
the more peaceful we are, the more happy we are.

14
00:01:38,858 --> 00:01:49,691
And that's the base upon which we begin to investigate the three characteristics of existence, impermanence, the cause of suffering, desire, and not-self.

15
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:56,459
That's what brings about the end of delusion, which is, of course, a fundamental problem.

16
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:57,719
Okay.

17
00:01:58,341 --> 00:02:01,986
So we have three positions.

18
00:02:02,006 --> 00:02:10,536
We've got nihilism, the dark side of nihilism, just not obeying anything, not caring for anything.

19
00:02:11,156 --> 00:02:17,323
And then we have the idea that there's no consequence to karma, no consequence to your actions.

20
00:02:18,485 --> 00:02:23,370
So there's no moral law, really, apart from getting away with it.

21
00:02:23,569 --> 00:02:27,975
And the third one was causality, that everything is fate.

22
00:02:28,657 --> 00:02:42,835
And the Buddha says, no, we have this one little moment where we can see a desire and we have the ability to say, no, if it's unwholesome, let it die out, empty the heart of that negativity over time.

23
00:02:43,856 --> 00:02:46,841
And we can say yes, whenever there's something wholesome.

24
00:02:47,502 --> 00:02:50,885
And that's the great effort.

25
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:51,687
Okay.

26
00:02:55,260 --> 00:03:06,060
So these people always end up with bad news and they don't realize the drawbacks, the wretchedness, the corruption of what they're doing.

27
00:03:07,322 --> 00:03:13,413
And they don't get the benefits of renunciation, the cleansing power and the benefit of renunciation.

28
00:03:13,432 --> 00:03:15,717
Remember, renunciation is not self-mortification.

29
00:03:16,237 --> 00:03:18,602
It's giving up those wrong desires.

30
00:03:19,914 --> 00:03:36,433
And then the usual thing, because of this wrong speech, you get wrong thought, sorry, because of this wrong view, you get wrong thought, wrong speech, contradicting the noble ones, convincing others to accept untrue teaching and glorifying yourself and putting others down.

31
00:03:38,276 --> 00:03:45,424
And then there's an old argument, well, if there is no world, okay, they're safe in the sense that when they die, nothing's going to happen.

32
00:03:45,905 --> 00:03:47,847
But if there is, bad news.

33
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:57,800
And then there's the argument, if we say there's no other world, even so, people will criticize them for their bad behavior.

34
00:03:58,941 --> 00:04:09,653
And of course, if there is a future world, they will lose, as it says on two counts, A, they're criticized in this life, and in the next life, they end up in a place of loss.

35
00:04:10,695 --> 00:04:16,062
So those are your sort of basic arguments put forward to people who have wrong view.

36
00:04:20,074 --> 00:04:27,764
So now then the discourse goes into a section which is not particularly pertinent to us, the formless states.

37
00:04:29,747 --> 00:04:38,119
These are the high absorption states where there's nothing to be seen, nothing to be known, etc.

38
00:04:38,459 --> 00:04:43,045
Just an empty space, experiences an empty space.

39
00:04:43,564 --> 00:04:47,151
And some people say there isn't, some people say there is, etc., etc.

40
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:49,754
So I don't think we need to worry too much about that.

41
00:04:49,774 --> 00:04:51,117
But the next one is important.

42
00:04:51,778 --> 00:04:56,625
There are some people who say that there is no cessation of future lives.

43
00:04:57,185 --> 00:05:02,593
Now, this means there's no nirvana, there's no transcendence.

44
00:05:02,654 --> 00:05:04,257
It's not the same as annihilation.

45
00:05:07,122 --> 00:05:17,113
So then a sensible person reflects in this manner, some ascetics and Brahmins say that there's no such thing as Nibbāna, but I have not seen that.

46
00:05:17,774 --> 00:05:25,302
And some ascetics and Brahmins say there is no such thing as Nibbāna, there is such a thing as Nibbāna, but I've not known that.

47
00:05:26,004 --> 00:05:35,475
Without knowing or seeing it, would it be appropriate for me to take one side and declare this is the truth and all the other ideas are silly?

48
00:05:36,315 --> 00:05:40,101
So here's the Buddha saying, you know, stay in that place of don't know.

49
00:05:40,141 --> 00:05:48,112
It's not a very comfortable place for us to be, you know, because we like to be certain, we like to know.

50
00:05:49,495 --> 00:05:56,586
So being able to remain in a place of don't know when we actually don't know is a truthful position.

51
00:05:57,387 --> 00:06:04,838
There's no need for us to come to a conclusion about future lives, about Nibbana and so on and so forth.

52
00:06:05,307 --> 00:06:09,331
What we do is we keep investigating to see whether it is true or not.

53
00:06:14,117 --> 00:06:16,841
Now, here's a rather subtle point, I think.

54
00:06:17,562 --> 00:06:25,331
Those who believe that rebirth will just be continuous, right?

55
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:31,278
There are some people who believe that you basically just keep being reborn, reborn, reborn forever.

56
00:06:31,619 --> 00:06:33,440
There was no end to it.

57
00:06:35,024 --> 00:06:38,668
Now, those Brahmins who say such a thing.

58
00:06:44,276 --> 00:06:44,958
It is right.

59
00:06:46,019 --> 00:06:46,360
Hold on.

60
00:06:46,519 --> 00:06:47,540
I've got myself lost here.

61
00:06:48,062 --> 00:06:50,444
Those to say that such a thing.

62
00:06:54,110 --> 00:06:54,451
Right.

63
00:06:54,810 --> 00:06:59,057
So those those Brahmins who say that is this continuous rebirthing.

64
00:06:59,543 --> 00:07:29,348
their lives are close to greed approving attachments and grasping so in other words uh this rebirthing process is um engineered is compelled by this sense of self and the sense of self of me being this person being this human being so when i die i might aspire to be something higher a god or something or because of bad actions i might end up as a pig so

65
00:07:30,322 --> 00:07:40,836
whatever I end up as, it's always going to be driven by this sense of self, this sense of wanting to keep becoming.

66
00:07:41,357 --> 00:07:43,459
And that's the grasping and that's the suffering.

67
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:52,550
So those who believe in a continuous life form will never come to the end of greed, aversion, fear, and delusion.

68
00:07:53,831 --> 00:08:00,279
Now, those who say there's no such thing as the end, as nirvana,

69
00:08:00,817 --> 00:08:04,880
They are close to non-greed, non-approval, non-attachment.

70
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:18,392
That's interesting, you see, in the sense that those who believe that there will be just annihilation are close to giving everything up because at some point they are going to have to do that.

71
00:08:19,252 --> 00:08:30,283
And if there is an afterlife, if there is nirvana, they will slowly discover it because of that process of renouncing things just by getting old, just by dying.

72
00:08:37,131 --> 00:08:43,241
So that's the argument for the cessation, cessation of becoming as it's put.

73
00:08:43,942 --> 00:08:48,110
And then the final thing is just about the four types of persons found in the world.

74
00:08:52,636 --> 00:09:02,092
So the first one is those people who just torment themselves and are committed to the practice of tormenting and mortifying themselves.

75
00:09:04,874 --> 00:09:11,461
And there are those people who are committed to mortifying others and tormenting others.

76
00:09:13,241 --> 00:09:20,610
And then there are those people who are committed to mortifying both themselves and others, to tormenting themselves and others.

77
00:09:20,710 --> 00:09:28,697
And finally, of course, there is the person who neither torments themselves nor others.

78
00:09:29,317 --> 00:09:31,440
They live without wishes in the present life.

79
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:34,222
They're extinguished, cooled, experiencing bliss,

80
00:09:34,725 --> 00:09:36,927
having become holy in themselves.

81
00:09:37,248 --> 00:09:40,171
And they are, of course, the fully liberated persons.

82
00:09:44,475 --> 00:09:55,446
And then there's the usual business of how you do that, in this case through the absorptions, et cetera, et cetera.

83
00:09:55,466 --> 00:09:58,509
And they come to realize that there is an end to suffering.

84
00:10:01,111 --> 00:10:03,214
Now, of course, when all this is spoken,

85
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:28,341
the Brahmins of Sala, who were in a quandary as to who to believe and who not to believe, end off with this paean, of course, to the Buddha, excellent master Gautama, excellent, as if he were writing the overturn or revealing the hidden, pointing out the path to the lost, lighting a lamp in the dark so people with good eyes can see,

86
00:10:29,366 --> 00:10:32,350
Master Gautama has made the teaching clear in many ways.

87
00:10:32,669 --> 00:10:37,634
We go for refuge to Master Gautama, to the teaching, to the mendicant Sangha.

88
00:10:37,894 --> 00:10:44,642
From this day forth, may Master Gautama remember us as lay disciples who've gone forth for refuge, for life.

89
00:10:45,383 --> 00:10:47,924
So that's another win for the Buddha.

90
00:10:49,966 --> 00:10:59,255
So these arguments, these points that are made about nihilism, etc., etc.,

91
00:10:59,455 --> 00:11:06,721
The scriptures often are, shall we say, very basic in their explanation.

92
00:11:07,202 --> 00:11:09,024
That's why there are these commentaries.

93
00:11:09,565 --> 00:11:23,860
There are commentaries on the commentaries, the Tikka, and these days, of course, there are as many writers as you wish, both academic and practitioners, who continue to

94
00:11:24,211 --> 00:11:28,174
give their view upon these teachings by the Buddha.

95
00:11:28,195 --> 00:11:38,325
So it's a never-ending process, really, of using the scriptures in order to develop what is meaningful to us in our own lives.

96
00:11:39,066 --> 00:11:42,370
So if we take the scriptures at that level, then they work for us.

97
00:11:44,091 --> 00:11:51,619
So I can only hope that going through this discourse has been somewhat inspiring and that you

98
00:11:51,851 --> 00:12:16,779
will continue with your practice until that very day when you will be fully liberated, hopefully sooner rather than later.

